  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x² + 0.8 z²)/2, drawn at 0.4 × loss over x, z from -2 to 2. It is steeper in x, so overshoot shows there first. Steps diverge for learning rates above 0.8 (2/2.5). At exactly 0.8 the swings in x stay the same size.",
    "Each step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian, centred on zero, with SD 1.5·√((64−B)/(63·B)) for batch size B. That is 1.5 at B=1 and exactly 0 at B=64. With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "The arrows are drawn at their true lengths (scale 1). Orange is the mini-batch step the ball will take next, grey is the full-data step −lr × true slope. Both are hidden after a run ends, and the step length then reads 0.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or as diverged when the loss goes above 6.6 or the ball leaves the drawn square. Off-surface positions are drawn at the edge, but the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The ball makes 5 steps per second while running. The trail holds the start point plus up to 40 landings. Noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence, while each new run uses new noise values."
  ]
}